Now VideoPlayerActivity zoom. Modify Overlay.onTouchEvent + fields + applyTransform, and tap scheduling.

Add outer fields:
```java
private float zoom = 1f, panX = 0f, panY = 0f;
private float pinch0, zoom0, mid0X, mid0Y, pan0X, pan0Y;
private float lastX, lastY, downX, downY;
private boolean multi, moved;
private final Runnable tapAction = ... // scheduled single tap
```
For the single-tap delay, use a field `Runnable pendingTap` and Handler.

Implementation in Overlay.onTouchEvent:

```java
int am = e.getActionMasked();
switch(am) {
 case DOWN: downX=lastX=e.getX(); ... multi = false; moved=false; pinch0=0; zoom0=zoom; pan0X=panX; ...
 case POINTER_DOWN: multi=true; pinch0=dist; mid0..., zoom0, pan0...
 case MOVE: 
   if (count>=2 && pinch0>1) { zoom = clamp(zoom0*d/pinch0,1,5); r; pan = ...; applyTransform(); overlay.invalidate(); }
   else if (zoom>1.001) { panX += dx; panY += dy; applyTransform(); }
   if (moved beyond 14) moved=true;
   lastX/lastY
 case POINTER_UP: if remaining 1 reset last, pinch0=0
 case UP:
   if (!multi && !moved && zoom<=1.001) scheduleTap(x,y)  // 260ms 延迟以区分双击
   multi=false
}
```
But overlay's `ui` control visibility: currently any touch calls wakeControls. With gestures, dragging shouldn't necessarily reset the hider timer... acceptable to keep controls shown while zoomed? Let's call wakeControls() only on tap.

Careful: when controls hidden (ui=false), onDraw returns early but touch still works — good, and tapping should wake controls. Currently single tap toggles play. Keep.

Hmm if controls hidden and user wants to pinch, works without waking.

scheduleTap: 
```java
private void scheduleTap(final float x, final float y) {
   cancel pending
   pendingTap = () -> { pendingTap=null; handleTap(x,y); }
   h.postDelayed(pendingTap, 240);
}
```
handleTap(x,y): if double-tap within window... wait, double tap detection: first tap schedules handleTap after 240ms; a second DOWN/UP within 240ms → we can detect by checking elapsed since lastTapAt inside tap scheduling:

```java
private long lastTapAt; private float lastTapX, lastTapY;
private void scheduleTap(float x, float y) {
    long now = System.currentTimeMillis();
    if (now - lastTapAt < 260 && Math.abs(x-lastTapX)<60 && Math.abs(y-lastTapY)<60) {
        lastTapAt = 0;
        if (pendingTap != null) { h.removeCallbacks(pendingTap); pendingTap = null; }
        // 双击：切换缩放
        if (zoom > 1.001f) { zoom=1; panX=panY=0; applyTransform(); }
        else { zoomTo(2.5f, x, y); }
        overlay.invalidate();
        return;
    }
    lastTapAt = now; lastTapX = x; lastTapY = y;
    if (pendingTap != null) h.removeCallbacks(pendingTap);
    pendingTap = () -> { pendingTap = null; singleTap(x, y); };
    h.postDelayed(pendingTap, 240);
}
```
singleTap = existing action logic (close btn / seek / toggle play / wake).

Since the second tap's UP comes at ~200ms maybe > 240? Double tap typically <300ms, first UP to second UP ~200-250ms. Risky. Increase delay to 280ms. Actual double tap interval usually 150-250ms. Use 260ms and check `now - lastTapAt < 320`.

Note that when the first tap is on the seek bar, the delayed action feels laggy but acceptable (260ms).

applyTransform: set scale/translation on surface. Also when zoom changes need clamp with current surface size (which is set by video size). Use getWidth/getHeight; if 0 (not laid out), skip.

Add zoom badge to overlay draw (top-right "2.0×"). And hint? maybe show "双指缩放" only briefly. Add badge when zoom>1.

Also when zoomed, video continues.

Implement by rewriting VideoPlayerActivity fully? It's 261 lines; easier to patch specific parts. Let me do a full rewrite for clarity and safety.

