Now: after exit, the app UI content (title "腕上管家", storage bar, tile entries) still shows briefly — meaning the last presented buffer when finishing is the app UI at ex=0! Why? Because during exitAnim we use invalFast() — maybe invalFast doesn't actually trigger a full redraw presentation? Wait but drag itself shows the disc moving — user sees it slide out. Then desktop appears... then app UI flashes back. So the last presented frame was app UI fully centered?!

Hmm — maybe the exitAnim drawing happens but presentation of later frames fails? Or: the flash comes from the **task snapshot** taken by launcher's RecentsTaskLoadPlan (we saw preloadPlan right after close). Launcher shows task snapshot as part of its "close app" animation — the snapshot is of the app UI (taken when... maybe when dragging began? snapshot at ex=0 since drag frames are translucent?).

Actually wait: v1.22 made window translucent from onCreate, so snapshot should be skipped. Unless ROM snapshots anyway.

Alternative: the flash is launcher's own transition: OPPO wearable launcher "close app" animation = zoom out from app screenshot. That screenshot is taken at close time by system (TaskSnapshot) — but translucent...

Hold on — simpler: is the flash actually the **app's window appearing at ex=0 again**? Why would it? After our postDelayed finish... during those 80ms, the window shows transparent, then finish. Then launcher. Then flash of app UI. If the flash is a snapshot, disabling snapshots: `android:freezesSnapshots`? Not public.

Try different approach: instead of finishing while translucent, first present a fully transparent frame (done) — but the flash shows full app UI, i.e., a *different* surface: likely the task snapshot surface or launcher's animation. To defeat snapshot: FLAG_SECURE on the window makes snapshots black (but that'd flash black). Hmm.

Wait — maybe the snapshot was taken at an earlier point, e.g., when the app was paused... it shows title + tiles + storage bar = normal home screen. So a snapshot of the home screen exists somewhere and gets displayed after our window leaves.

Where does it come from? On Android 11, when finishing an activity, WM may show a **"closing window" animation using the app window itself**; with 0,0 no anim. The launcher's RecentsTaskLoadPlan preload right after suggests launcher refreshed recents — the flash might be launcher's **task view thumbnail** briefly?? Odd.

Better idea: capture it! Use screenrecord but the user says recording may not catch. We can also use `adb shell screenrecord --time-limit` high fps. Or use SurfaceFlinger: `adb shell dumpsys SurfaceFlinger --latency`? Too complex.

Alternative empirical approach: try `finishAndRemoveTask()`; try also removing our window's app snapshot by calling `setTaskDescription`? no.

Another practical trick used by apps: instead of finish, animate then call `moveTaskToBack(true)`... flashed earlier.

Actually, maybe the flash IS our app window: when finishing, if there are pending messages... wait, exitHold: after exitAnim end, ex=to, invalidate() — but do we ever reset homeExitDrag? At ACTION_UP: homeExitDrag=false, then finishHomeExit. So during exitAnim, seeThrough relies on exitAnim!=null; during hold, exitHold. OK.

But WAIT: does the onDraw actually get called during the hold with seeThrough? `invalidate()` — but earlier code: "invalidate() 在 animating||popDx>0 时冻结圆盘快照" — there's logic in invalidate() override? ShellView might override invalidate() to snapshot disc. If discStale handling causes drawing the *snapshot* rather than skipping? Need to see onDraw around line 352 and draw466 with drawCircled(c, cur, ex). When seeThrough, drawCircled draws the disc at offset ex — ex=W so offscreen. Fine.

Hmm, but the user's flash shows the FULL UI — disc at ex=0. Which frame looks like that? The frame right before the drag started (ex=0)! I.e., the LAST OPAQUE content frame. So the presented buffer sequence: during drag, frames show disc moving... but did those frames actually present? User saw disc sliding — yes visible. Unless what user saw sliding was... they did see desktop behind. So presented frames updated during drag.

Then at end, desktop appears (fully transparent frame presented). Then flash of app UI at ex=0. So after fully transparent, something displays an OLD frame (ex=0, full UI). That is 100% consistent with the system's **task close animation playing a snapshot** OR the surface releasing and compositor falling back to an earlier buffer.

Android's "remove window" with 0,0: WM removes window immediately. If our app's surface had multiple pending buffers, the compositor wouldn't resurrect old ones.

Strong suspicion: **OPPO launcher plays an app-close animation using TaskSnapshot** (that's why RecentsTaskLoadPlan appears). The snapshot was taken when? TaskSnapshotController takes snapshot when app becomes invisible... at that point our window content = fully transparent. But maybe ROM's launcher keeps its OWN snapshot from app's previous pause (when user launched another app earlier) — cached, shows stale home UI.

Countermeasure: make the snapshot useless: set FLAG_SECURE? snapshot black → black flash. Bad.

Countermeasure 2: don't let the task "close" visually — keep the app window alive but empty: instead of finish(), keep activity but make window fully transparent AND not touchable, then... the launcher is behind but task still on top; user is "on desktop" but back button... hacky.

Countermeasure 3: use `finishAfterTransition`? no.

Countermeasure 4: The nuclear option that definitely avoids any system snapshot animation: **don't finish at all — instead launch the launcher and finish while occluded**. E.g., Intent to home (CATEGORY_HOME) → app goes background (snapshot taken? we're transparent already) → then finish quietly in background. moveTaskToBack flashed earlier because at that time window wasn't translucent and ex reset... Now with v1.23 hold (fully transparent presented), `moveTaskToBack(true)` might be seamless: system plays task-switch animation from our transparent window to launcher = invisible. Then finish in background (postDelayed 500ms) — finishing a background task has no visual.

But careful: earlier summary said moveTaskToBack flashed a snapshot — that was v1.20 without transparent presented frame. Now we present transparency first.

Let's implement: in the postDelayed: instead of finish immediately, do `((MainActivity) ctx).moveTaskToBack(true)` then postDelayed finish 400ms. Actually if we moveTaskToBack and then finish, total visual: transparent window → task goes back → launcher. Then finish removes task silently.

Hmm, but system "task to back" may also animate with snapshot... With 0,0-ish? moveTaskToBack has no animation params; Android may animate switch. Since our window shows fully transparent, the switch animation starting from transparent = invisible.

Also alternative: send user home via Intent:
```
Intent i = new Intent(Intent.ACTION_MAIN).addCategory(Intent.CATEGORY_HOME);
i.setFlags(FLAG_ACTIVITY_NEW_TASK);
startActivity(i);
overridePendingTransition(0,0);
```
then postDelayed finish. This is a common trick for seamless exit.

Combine: present transparent frame → startActivity(HOME) with 0,0 transitions → finish after delay. Let me implement that replacing moveTaskToBack path.

Risk: launcher picks task... fine.

